00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
In the last video, we talked about exponentially weighted averages.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000
This will turn out to be a key component of several optimization algorithms that you use to train your neural networks.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.000
So in this video, I want to delve a little bit deeper into intuitions for what this algorithm is really doing.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Recall that this is the key equation for implementing exponentially weighted averages.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
And so if beta equals 0.9, you got the red line.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
If it was much closer to 1, it was 0.98.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
You get the green line. And if it's much smaller, maybe 0.5, you get the yellow line.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Let's look at a bit more at the math to understand how this is computing averages of the daily temperature.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.000
So here's that equation again.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
And let's set beta equals 0.9 and write out a few equations that this corresponds to.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.000
So whereas when implementing it, you'd have t going from 0 to 1 to 2 to 3 increasing values of t.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
To analyze it, I've written it with decreasing values of t.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.000
And this goes on.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
So let's take this first equation here and understand what V100 really is.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
So V100 is going to be, let me reverse these two terms.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
This is going to be 0.1 times theta 100 plus 0.9 times whatever the value was on the previous day.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:24.000
But what is V99? Well, we just plugged it in from this equation.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
So this is just going to be 0.1 times theta 99.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
And again, I've reversed these two terms plus 0.9 times V98.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.000
But then what is V98? Well, you just get that from here.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:52.000
So you can just plug in here 0.1 times theta 98 plus 0.9 times V97 and so on.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:00.000
And if you multiply all of these terms out, you can show that V100 is 0.1 times theta 100 plus.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Now let's look at coefficient on theta 99.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:09.000
It's going to be 0.1 times 0.9 times theta 99.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Now let's look at the coefficient on theta 98.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.000
There's a 0.1 here times 0.9 times 0.9.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:27.000
So if you expand out the algebra, this becomes 0.1 times 0.9 squared times theta 98.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:38.000
And if you keep expanding this out, you find that this becomes 0.1 times 0.9 cubed, theta 97 plus 0.1 times 0.9 to the 4th times theta 96.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Plus dot, dot, dot. So this is really a weighted sum, a thus a weighted average of theta 100,

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.000
which is the current day's temperature. We're looking for a perspective of V100, which you calculate on the hundredth day of the year.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:02.000
But so it's a sum of theta 100, theta 99, theta 98, theta 97, theta 96, and so on.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:09.000
So one way to draw this in pictures would be if, let's say we have some number of days of temperature.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:18.000
So this is theta and this is T. So theta 100 would be some value, then theta 99 would be some value, theta 98.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:26.000
So these are, so this is T equals 100, 99, 98, and so on. So you have some number of days of temperature.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:44.000
And what we have is then an exponentially decaying function. So starting from 0.1 to 0.9 times 0.1 to 0.9 squared times 0.1 to, and so on.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:54.000
So you have this exponentially decaying function. And the way you compute V100 is you take the element-wise product between these two functions

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:08.000
and sum it up. So you take this value, theta 100 times 0.1, times this value, theta 99 times 0.1 times 0.9, that's the second term, and so on.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.000
So it's really taking the daily temperature, multiplying with this exponentially decaying function, and then summing it up.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:32.000
And this becomes your V100. It turns out that up to details, I'll talk about later, but all of these coefficients add up to 1, or add up to very close to 1, up to a detail called bias correction, which I'll talk about in the next video.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:41.000
But because of that, this really is an exponentially weighted average. And finally, you might wonder, you know, how many days temperature is this averaging over?

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Well, it turns out that 0.9, right, to the power of 10, is about 0.35, and this turns out to be about 1 over E, one of the base of natural logarithms.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:08.000
And more generally, if you have 1 minus epsilon, so in this example, epsilon would be 0.1, so if this was 0.9, then 1 minus epsilon to the 1 over epsilon, this is about 1 over E.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:24.000
This is about 0.34, 0.35. And so, in other words, it takes about 10 days for the height of this to decay to, you know, around one third, already 1 over E of the peak.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:40.000
So it's because of this that when beta equals 0.9, we say that it takes, this is as if you're computing an exponentially weighted average that focuses on just the last 10 days temperature,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:48.000
because it's sort of after 10 days, that the weight decays to, you know, less than about a third of the weight of the current day.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:59.000
Whereas in contrast, if beta was equal to 0.98, then well, what do you need 0.98 to the power of in order for this to be really small?

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:07.000
It turns out that 0.98 to the power of 50 will be approximately equal to 1 over E, so the weight will be pretty big.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.000
It will be bigger than 1 over E for the first 50 days, and then it will decay quite rapidly over that.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:25.000
So intuitively, this is a hard and fast thing. You can think of this as averaging over about 50 days temperature, because in this example, to use the notation here on the left is as if epsilon is equal to 0.02.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:35.000
So 1 over epsilon is 50. And this, by the way, is how we got the formula that we're averaging over 1 over 1 minus beta, also days.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Right here, epsilon, we place a row of 1 minus beta. It tells you up to some constant roughly how many days temperature you should think of this as averaging over.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.000
But this is just a rule of thumb for how to think about it and is in a formal mathematical statement.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:07:01.000
Finally, let's talk about how you actually implement this. Recall that we start off with V0, initialize a 0, then you compute V1 on the first day, V2, and so on.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:09.000
Now, to explain the algorithm, it was useful to write down V0, V1, V2, and so on as distinct variables.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:15.000
But if you're implementing this in practice, this is what you do. You initialize V to be equal to 0.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:25.000
And then on day 1, you would set V equals beta times V plus 1 minus beta times theta 1.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:35.000
And then on the next day, you would update V to be equal to beta V plus 1 minus beta theta 2, and so on.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:46.000
And sometimes we use this notation V subscript theta to denote that V is computing this exponentially weighted average of the parameter theta.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:08:01.000
So just to say this again, but in a for loop format, you set V theta equals 0, and then repeatedly, and if on each day, you would get next theta t,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:12.000
and then set V theta gives updated as beta times the old value of V theta plus 1 minus beta times the current value of V theta.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.000
So one of the advantages of this exponentially weighted average formula is that it takes very little memory.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:27.000
You just need to keep this one real number in computing memory, and you keep on overwriting it with this formula based on the latest value that you got.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:38.000
And it's really this reason, the efficiency, it just takes one line of code basically, and just storage and memory for a single real number to compute this exponentially weighted average.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.000
It's really not the best way, not the most accurate way to compute an average.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:53.000
If you were to compute a moving window where you explicitly sum over the last 10 days, the last 50 days temperature, and just divide by 10 or divide by 50, that usually gives you a better estimate.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:09:04.000
But the disadvantage of that, of explicitly keeping all the temperatures around and summing the last 10 days, is it requires more memory, and it's just more complicated to implement, and it's computationally more expensive.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:12.000
So for things, we'll see some examples in the next few videos, where you need to compute averages of a lot of variables.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:20.000
This is a very efficient way to do so, both from a computation and memory efficiency point of view, which is why it's used a lot in machine learning.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.000
Not to mention that it's just one line of code, which is maybe another advantage.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.000
So now you know how to implement exponentially weighted averages.

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:33.000
There's one more technical detail that's worth you knowing about called bias correction.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:41.000
Let's see that in the next video. And then after that, you'll be able to use this to build a better optimization algorithm than the straightforward gradient descent.
